tools.description:
USE WHEN you need to look up crypto-agent tooling (frameworks, MCP servers, wallets, payment rails, data tools) by name, chain, standard or what it does, or check that a specific project exists and is maintained. A daily-rebuilt directory, each entry scored from public evidence and linking to a citable page.
NOT FOR a build goal in plain words such as "a trading agent on Base" (use onchain_agent_recommend_stack instead), a RUNNING agent to hire or pay (use onchain_agent_search_agents), a downloadable agent package (use onchain_agent_search_listings), what an agent skill touches (use onchain_agent_search_skills), or one record by slug (use onchain_agent_get_resource). `search` is the one-box version for ChatGPT deep research.

RETURNS (json): { total, count, offset, has_more, next_offset?, resources: [...] }; each resource has chains, status, liveness, github_stars, verification_status and the marketplace fields (is_agent/is_hirable/is_licensable). Read-only. With NO argument it is a compact overview (rule "defaulted": complete rows, fewer, plus directory counts); detail: "full" is the full first page.

FILTERS: query (free text, AND-matched terms), chain, status, liveness, featured, and the taxonomy facets (docs/taxonomy.md) entity_class, resource_type, use_case, standard and iface — closed vocabularies listed in the input schema: anything else is refused with the list, never answered with an empty result. Legacy flags still work: category, is_agent, is_skill, is_harness. Sort by priority (default), newest_release, stars, or name. Paginates via limit/offset. Deprecated resources are never returned.

STARTING POINTS: a query asking for somewhere to start — "tutorial", "starter", "beginner", "template", "example", "docs" — returns starting points labelled as such: listings named as a starter or template first, then listings whose install Sato Hub reproduced, plus the wiki guides. "starter" / "template" / "x402 starter" also carry `templates`: a pointer to onchain_agent_create_agent and the templates catalogue (never a result), unless the query names another chain or runtime. Only when the catalogue has no template for Base is it `starter` instead: a TypeScript template built by Sato Hub (labelled ours, not audited, never a result). The directory lists tools, not tutorials, and says so.

Examples:
  - "Active hirable agents on Base" -> { chain: "Base", is_agent: true, liveness: "Active" }
  - "newest releases" -> { sort: "newest_release", limit: 10 }
  - "wallet tooling" -> { query: "wallet" }
  - "MCP servers I can use from Claude Code" -> { integration: "claude code", iface: "mcp" }
  - "things I can run in Docker" -> { deploys_as: "docker" }
  - "MCP servers that actually answer" -> { mcp_answering: true } (the MCP endpoint answered our last handshake)
  - "...with a sustained record" -> { mcp_answering: true, min_observed_success: 95 }
  - "only installs Sato Hub has reproduced" -> { verified_only: true }

USE WHEN you keep a local copy of the directory and need only what moved since your last sync (machine-readable added / updated / removed), rather than re-fetching the whole catalog.
NOT FOR a readable headline feed or one project's history (use onchain_agent_recent_changes instead), or the current state of one record (use onchain_agent_get_resource).

RETURNS (json): what changed since a given date: additions, per-resource change events, and retirements. { since, until, window_days, next_since, counts:{added,changed,removed}, added:[...], changed:[{slug,name,url,events:[...]}], removed:[...], coverage:{...}, full_export }.

CURSOR: `next_since` is the date to pass as `since` on your next call. It starts the next window at a snapshot taken before this read, so a change cannot fall between two calls; it overlaps by a couple of days, so dedupe by slug. To watch a fixed list of listings, call on a schedule with `since` = the last `next_since` and look for your slugs in each lane. A call with no argument returns a truncated sample: take the cursor from a call with `since` or detail "full", not from the sample.

COVERAGE (also stated in the response): `added` is exact. `updated` is a field-level diff of every catalog field between the snapshot on `baseline_date` and the live record — complete at daily resolution, so a quiet copy edit IS caught. `changed` is the richer event log (score moves, releases, verification) and explains WHY. `removed` is approximate. Mirror from added + updated + removed. Read-only.

WITH NO `since` (and no detail: "full") the answer is compact, labelled rule "defaulted": exact counts for the default 14-day window and the newest rows of each lane (summary: true, shown, omitted, order, example_calls). It is a sample, not a sync. Pass `since` (any date) or detail: "full" for every row; a later `since` is a shorter window and a smaller answer. All free.

Examples:
  - "what moved recently" -> {} (compact) · every row -> { detail: "full" }
  - "what's new this week" -> { since: "2026-08-23" }
  - "sync my copy" or "watch my list" -> { since: <the next_since of your last call> }

USE WHEN asked how big the onchain agent economy actually is (how many agents are really registered, launched, paying or trading) and you want measured chain reads instead of a figure from a deck or an announcement. Covers the agent venues Sato Hub tracks: registries, launchpads, payment rails and account infrastructure, measured weekly from public chain reads.
NOT FOR a series over time (use onchain_agent_get_trend instead), one headline ERC-8004 count (use onchain_agent_get_metrics), or agents registered with Sato Hub (use onchain_agent_search_agents).

RETURNS (json): { week, as_of, rules, evidence_tiers, venues: [{ id, name, unit, entry_cost, measurable, overlaps_with, headline_safe, contracts:[...], chains:[{ chain, stages:[{ stage, value, unit, method, evidence_tier, sample_size, denominator, covered_days, publishable, caveat }] }], platforms:[...] }] }. With no venue, chain or include_platforms (and no detail: "full") it is a compact summary instead, labelled rule "defaulted": { summary: true, week, as_of, rules, venues: [{ id, name, venue_unit, headline_safe, overlaps_with?, stage, unit, caveat, as_of, readings: [{ chain, value, sample_size, publishable, evidence_tier, unit?, caveat? }], more_chains, paired_stage? (the second stage of a venue quotable only as a two-stage figure, same shape), other_stages, full_detail: { tool, args } }], full_detail }. The week is the payload's, one for every venue; each stage's unit and caveat are its own rows', not the venue's.

HOW TO USE THESE NUMBERS. Never add them together: an ERC-8004 registration, an Olas staked service, a Virtuals launch and a Mech task are four different objects, and each venue's `unit` says which. Every number names its stage — "19,180 launched, 1,233 graduated" is true, "58,400 agents" is not. A null value means UNKNOWN, never zero. A rate whose sample_size is below 20 is returned with `publishable: false` and should not be quoted. Solana identity registries are covered as UPPER BOUNDS (program-account counts, the unit says so); Solana payment settlement is not covered by any row, and by transaction count x402 mostly settles there.

Read-only. Cite https://satohub.ai/agent-economy.

Examples:
  - "how many agents are actually registered onchain" -> {} (summary) · every row -> { detail: "full" }
  - "what is happening on Base" -> { chain: "Base" }
  - "who is producing ERC-8004 registrations" -> { venue: "erc8004", include_platforms: true }

USE WHEN the question is whether a measurement is going up or down (ANSWERS ONE QUESTION). Returns a bounded series (at most 30 points, default 8) for ONE venue, ONE chain and ONE stage from Sato Hub's own weekly (or daily) chain reads, each point with its date, value, sample_size and method.
NOT FOR this week's numbers across venues (use onchain_agent_get_agent_economy instead) or one listing's score or check history (use onchain_agent_get_listing_history).

Pass stage (required). Venue is optional: name it to read exactly one series; omit it and a stage that only one venue reports is read from that venue, while a stage several venues report comes back as one SEPARATE series per venue. Stages by venue: erc8004 reports registered (weekly), registered_new_1d (daily), active_7d, mcp_endpoint_answers; olas reports registered and working; virtuals reports launched; virtuals_acp reports jobs_created; x402 reports settled_to_catalogued_seller, answers_402 (weekly) and facilitator_txs_1d_known (daily). A stage name alone is enough when one venue reports it; when several do (registered is reported by more than one venue) you get one separate series per venue.

RULE ENFORCED: points are never summed across venues, chains or stages — the tool refuses to blend chains and tells you which chains exist when you omit one, and several venues are never combined. A null point is UNKNOWN for that period, never zero. The weekly point is the citable one; daily points feed a trend and never a headline. Raw rows are not returned.

RETURNS (json): { venue, chain, stage, grain, unit, points: [{ date, value, sample_size, method, evidence_tier }], direction_first_to_last, known_points, rules, as_of, source } — or, when a stage several venues report is asked without a venue, { stage, grain, series: [that same object per venue], needs_chain?, no_reading?, rules }. Read-only.

Examples:
  - "are ERC-8004 registrations on Base growing" -> { venue: "erc8004", chain: "Base", stage: "registered" }
  - "how is registered trending on Base" (no venue) -> { stage: "registered", chain: "Base" }
  - "x402 sellers week over week" -> { venue: "x402", chain: "Base", stage: "settled_to_catalogued_seller", points: 12 }
  - "do registered endpoints answer, day by day" -> { venue: "erc8004", chain: "Base", stage: "mcp_endpoint_answers", grain: "daily", points: 30 }
